GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
ARCHIVE TRANSFER TEAM
ARCHIVE TRANSFER STAGING PLAN

Closure-period transfer material is to be assembled on the Records Trolley by batch.

Sequence:
1. withdraw holding and place marker in cabinet;
2. compare reference with transfer list;
3. place holding in labelled box;
4. prepare manifest;
5. obtain Senior Archivist verification;
6. seal box;
7. record handover.

The trolley is a controlled staging point, not an alternative filing system.

Normal refiling and transfer staging must not share the same divider.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
ARCHIVE TRANSFER AUTHORITY
SENIOR ARCHIVIST VERIFICATION ASSIGNMENT

For the final closure period, the Senior Archivist is personally assigned to verify:

- physical transfer exceptions;
- custody signatures;
- final transfer-box contents;
- digital holdings comparison;
- any variance between cabinet, trolley and manifest.

Restricted-area access may be requested where necessary to complete digital verification or inspect systems supporting the archive.

This assignment is a control measure, not a change of post.

The Senior Archivist is not to delegate final signature to staff whose work is being verified.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
SITE CLOSURE PROJECT
FINAL CLOSURE EXCEPTION SUMMARY

Following the suppression-system discharge and evacuation, the planned Friday handover is suspended.

Open matters now include:
- incident review by Operational Security;
- confirmation of all personnel and restricted-area access;
- completion of the archive reconciliation workstream;
- completion of the terminal-network remediation workstream;
- confirmation that essential server services remain stable.

No material is to be destroyed, transferred off Site or reclassified until the exception review is complete.

Project Manager:
"Friday's deadline has ceased to be the most important thing that happened on Friday."

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
SITE CLOSURE PROJECT
OPERATIONAL SECURITY REVIEW REQUIREMENT

The Site will remain under controlled closure pending a formal review of the suppression discharge.

Security Office is to assemble:
- access records;
- visitor records;
- attendance returns;
- evacuation accountability;
- duty communications;
- CCTV coverage and relevant retained footage.

The Secretary's Office is to preserve Director correspondence, appointment material and incident-related incoming messages.

This review is fact-finding. No individual is to be identified as responsible solely because they were authorised to be present.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
SITE CLOSURE PROJECT
REVISED HANDOVER CONDITIONS

The revised handover will require, in addition to the original closure controls:

- completed incident chronology;
- explanation of the suppression release input;
- confirmation of personnel and visitor movements;
- confirmation that archive exceptions are accounted for;
- confirmation that server and backup services are stable;
- Director approval after Security review.

The handover date is now "when complete".

This wording has been approved despite objections from the calendar.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
DIRECTOR'S OFFICE
CENTRALISATION PROGRAMME SUMMARY

The Department is reducing small local records sites and consolidating holdings, specialist staff and computing services.

Expected benefits:
- fewer duplicate catalogues;
- improved digital preservation;
- central custody of high-classification holdings;
- reduced building and support costs.

Known risks:
- temporary workload increase during transfer;
- overlap between physical and digital verification;
- dependence on ageing local systems during the final weeks.

The Site closure forms one scheduled part of this programme. It is not an exceptional shutdown.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
HUMAN RESOURCES
VACANCY & HOT-DESK PROVISION

Several posts have transferred ahead of Site closure. Their former desks may be used temporarily by closure staff, subject to the following:

- retain the original desk number;
- do not store classified material overnight;
- leave a written allocation slip;
- redirect calls through the current staff telephone list;
- clear personal property before handover.

Desk 04 is reserved for IT Support while the Main Office terminal fault remains open.

A hot desk is still a desk. HR has been asked to make this explicit after an argument nobody wishes to document twice.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
SITE DIRECTORY MAINTENANCE NOTE

The corridor Site Directory has been amended to reflect closure-period room use.

Remove:
- offices already vacated;
- obsolete team labels.

Retain:
- Reception;
- Main Office;
- Facilities;
- Records Office;
- IT Support;
- Security Office;
- Staff Room;
- Server Room;
- Director and Secretary accommodation.

Temporary paper labels are acceptable where permanent plates have already been scheduled for removal.

Please stop correcting the map with pencil. Facilities owns a ruler.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
FINAL WEEK WORK ORDER SUMMARY

OPEN:
- remove surplus furniture labels;
- inspect retained suppression controls;
- support terminal cable remediation;
- defer final service isolation until archive verification completes;
- final corridor signage removal after handover.

CLOSED:
- redundant office lighting circuits;
- non-essential storage clearance;
- contractor welfare arrangements.

HOLD:
- Server Room services;
- suppression system;
- main archive terminal supply.

Hold items require explicit release. Closure does not mean "switch off everything in alphabetical order."

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
RETAINED SERVICES SCHEDULE

RETAIN THROUGH FINAL HANDOVER:
- emergency lighting;
- access control;
- fire detection;
- Halon suppression serving the Server Room;
- Server Room power and ventilation;
- archive terminal power;
- essential network route;
- internal telephone service.

The Halon system is deliberately retained because the Server Room remains live for backup and verification work.

No retained service is to be isolated merely because its surrounding office is otherwise ready for closure.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
CONTRACTOR COORDINATION NOTE

Closure contractors will encounter staff still working in rooms that appear otherwise vacant.

Before work:
1. confirm room owner;
2. check HOLD tags;
3. identify shared cable or control routes;
4. agree any isolation;
5. record completion.

Particular caution: Server Room cable-riser work shares space with suppression-control wiring.

Contractors are not authorised to disconnect labelled control wiring unless the work order expressly includes it.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
FINAL ASSET SWEEP

The final sweep is to occur after operational work has ceased.

Check:
- desks and drawers;
- shelving;
- equipment labels;
- temporary work areas;
- cable reels and tools;
- transfer boxes;
- keys and access cards.

Do not remove items from an active incident or reconciliation area for tidiness.

A neat empty room is not the objective. An accountable room is.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
FACILITIES MANAGEMENT
POST-INCIDENT ENVIRONMENTAL CHECK

Server Room entry permitted only after atmosphere confirmation.

Post-event findings:
- no fire damage observed;
- no heat source identified;
- ventilation equipment serviceable after reset;
- Halon system discharged as designed;
- cable-riser work area left in active-work condition;
- control diagnostics retained for technical review.

Facilities recommendation:
Do not reset or reterminate the manual release circuit until IT and Security have documented the riser condition.

The room is safe to investigate, not safe to tidy.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
OPERATIONAL SECURITY
CONTRACTOR ACCESS AUTHORISATIONS

Facilities contractor access for final-week works:

Permitted:
- Facilities Office;
- agreed corridor routes;
- non-restricted plant/service areas under escort.

Not permitted without separate authority:
- Records Office;
- Security Office;
- Server Room;
- secure cabinets;
- archive transfer material.

Friday contractor activity concluded before midday.

The contractor's temporary pass was returned at Reception. No extension into the afternoon incident period is recorded.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
OPERATIONAL SECURITY
POST-EVACUATION SITE CHECK

Following atmosphere clearance, Security and Facilities conducted a limited preservation check.

Observed:
- no fire damage in Server Room;
- cable-riser work left incomplete;
- tools and pulled network cable present;
- suppression equipment discharged;
- relevant panels and paperwork left in place;
- no obvious forced entry to restricted rooms.

Nothing was repositioned except where required for safety.

The area was then held for technical examination.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
OPERATIONAL SECURITY
SECURE CABINET CODE HANDLING

Code-handling reminders for closure staff:

- never write a code on the cabinet;
- never include a code in an unclassified handover checklist;
- never reuse a room number as a code;
- destroy superseded working slips;
- report a suspected disclosure before changing anything;
- record the authorised change afterward.

Closure work creates more temporary paperwork than usual. It does not create a holiday from key control.

Security will provide current codes to authorised staff on request.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
TECHNOLOGY SERVICES
FINAL DIGITAL HOLDINGS VERIFICATION REPORT

Status: NOT COMPLETED

Verification media and checksum lists have been prepared. The Main Office archive terminal cannot currently reach the server holdings because of the open network-route fault.

Completed:
- verification set identified;
- media prepared;
- expected checksums printed;
- transfer labels prepared.

Outstanding:
- live comparison against server holdings;
- exception resolution;
- Senior Archivist confirmation.

No discrepancy in the holdings is asserted at this stage. The verification has not run.

GOVERNMENT DEPARTMENT OF RECORDS
TECHNOLOGY SERVICES
POST-DISCHARGE SYSTEM RECOVERY SUMMARY

Server services survived the suppression event and controlled re-entry.

Initial recovery:
- verify atmosphere safe;
- inspect rack power and UPS;
- preserve suppression diagnostic;
- leave cable-riser work undisturbed;
- confirm backups intact;
- prevent automatic overwrite of relevant logs.

No evidence of fire damage or server failure was found.

Technical investigation priority:
compare the manual-release wiring path with the exact network cable area being worked immediately before discharge.
